{"specification":{"scope":"Original WBXBAR public bus boundary, revision 2e8d3bc2d26ddc33d1881022a2a2b9d3f0c16b9b. Two masters, two slaves. No internal state or submodule observation.","authority":[{"source":"https://cdn.opencores.org/downloads/wbspec_b4.pdf","sha256":"d712d61fe7f363b671e6b821b7b0259f43ec0b4ce8f3b2072172b2878f347a5a","representation":"Maintainer paraphrase checked against PDF pages 31–38; not a new protocol specification.","requirements":[{"location":"3.1.1, rules 3.00–3.20, pp31–32","summary":"Reset takes effect on rising clock edges. Hold reset for at least one complete cycle. Master CYC and STB are inactive during reset; other master payload values are unspecified."},{"location":"3.1.2, rules 3.25/3.30, p33","summary":"CYC qualifies bus activity and spans a transfer cycle. Without CYC, slave request signals are inactive. System reset remains effective."},{"location":"3.1.3.2, rules 3.57–3.59, pp36–37; observation 3.10, p32","summary":"Pipelined acceptance requires CYC and STB high and STALL low. WE distinguishes writes from reads. New requests need not wait for earlier acknowledgments. ACK can occur while STB is low. Successful transactions retire with acknowledgments; read data is meaningful with ACK."},{"location":"permission 3.20 / rule 3.45, p35; rule 3.60, p37","summary":"ERR reports failure; the base protocol leaves the master reaction unspecified. ACK, ERR and RTY cannot coincide. Payload belongs to a strobed request. This interface has no RTY signal."}]},{"source":"third_party/wb2axip_candidate/README.md","sha256":"e21ffe5300b43bf2c38286ee8f2e201606d0a93f974b67f4c7235bd605904dd3","excerpts":[{"start_line":188,"end_line":196,"text":"188: - [WBXBAR](rtl/wbxbar.v) is a fully function N master to M slave Wishbone
189:   [crossbar](https://zipcpu.com/blog/2019/07/17/crossbar.html).  Unlike my
190:   Unlike my [earlier WB interconnects](https://zipcpu.com/blog/2017/06/22/simple-wb-interconnect.html), this one guarantees that the
191:   ACK responses won't get crossed, and that misbehaving slave accesses will
192:   be timed out.  The core also has options for checking for starvation
193:   (where a master's request is not granted in a particular period of time),
194:   double-buffering all outputs (i.e. with [skid
195:   buffers](https://zipcpu.com/blog/2019/05/22/skidbuffer.html), and forcing idle
196:   channel values to zero in order to reduce power."}]},{"source":"third_party/wb2axip_candidate/rtl/wbxbar.v","sha256":"d3fd1f2d7fb8909c2c2950c666e04c97d704c501c867201aedab5011ee106d2e","excerpts":[{"start_line":7,"end_line":39,"text":"7: // Purpose:	A Configurable wishbone cross-bar interconnect, conforming
8: //		to the WB-B4 pipeline specification, as described on the
9: //	ZipCPU blog.
10: //
11: // Performance:
12: //	Throughput: One transaction per clock
13: //	Latency: One clock to get access to an unused channel, another to
14: //	place the results on the slave bus, and another to return, or a minimum
15: //	of three clocks.
16: //
17: // Usage: To use, you'll need to set NM and NS to the number of masters
18: //	(input ports) and the number of slaves respectively.  You'll then
19: //	want to set the addresses for the slaves in the SLAVE_ADDR array,
20: //	together with the SLAVE_MASK array indicating which SLAVE_ADDRs
21: //	are valid.  Address and data widths should be adjusted at the same
22: //	time.
23: //
24: //	Voila, you are now set up!
25: //
26: //	Now let's fine tune this:
27: //
28: //	LGMAXBURST can be set to control the maximum number of outstanding
29: //	transactions.  An LGMAXBURST of 6 will allow 63 outstanding
30: //	transactions.
31: //
32: //	OPT_TIMEOUT, if set to a non-zero value, is a number of clock periods
33: //	to wait for a slave to respond.  Should the timeout expire and the
34: //	slave not respond, a bus error will be returned and the slave will
35: //	be issued a bus abort signal (CYC will be dropped).
36: //
37: //	OPT_STARVATION_TIMEOUT, if set, applies the OPT_TIMEOUT counter to
38: //	how long a particular master waits for arbitration.  If the master is
39: //	"starved", a bus error will be returned."}]},{"source":"https://zipcpu.com/blog/2019/07/17/crossbar.html","representation":"Prose-only intent paraphrase; no implementation examples, expressions or internal signal names included. Older design article is supplementary, not exact-cycle authority.","requirements":[{"location":"What is a Crossbar?; Returning data, paragraphs on nonexistent addresses and abort","summary":"A crossbar connects masters to slaves without mixing their responses. A request outside every configured address range should return an error. A master may end its cycle to abort outstanding work; completed data must not later be attributed to a new transaction. The article motivates aborting a cycle after an error. Exact buffering, arbitration and timeout edge counts require explicit documentation rather than assumptions."}]}],"parameters":{"NM":2,"NS":2,"AW":8,"DW":32,"SLAVE_ADDR":4096,"SLAVE_MASK":61680,"LGMAXBURST":3,"OPT_TIMEOUT":16,"OPT_STARVATION_TIMEOUT":1,"OPT_DBLBUFFER":1,"OPT_LOWPOWER":1},"interface":{"ports":{"inputs":{"i_reset":1,"i_mcyc":2,"i_mstb":2,"i_mwe":2,"i_maddr":16,"i_mdata":64,"i_msel":8,"i_sstall":2,"i_sack":2,"i_sdata":64,"i_serr":2},"outputs":{"o_mstall":2,"o_mack":2,"o_mdata":64,"o_merr":2,"o_scyc":2,"o_sstb":2,"o_swe":2,"o_saddr":16,"o_sdata":64,"o_ssel":8}},"packing":"Port 0 occupies the least-significant slice. Each address is an 8-bit bus address, each data value 32 bits, each select 4 bits. No implicit byte-address shifting.","address_configuration":"Slave0: (addr & 0xf0)==0x00; slave1: (addr & 0xf0)==0x10. Other addresses match neither slave. This is public parameter decoding, not stimulus guidance.","directions":"i_m* are external master inputs; o_m* are DUT responses. o_s* are DUT requests to external slaves; i_s* are external slave responses. i_clk is the common clock.","rows":"[{cycle:int, pre:{all listed inputs and outputs}, post:{all listed outputs}}]; integers of declared width. None may represent undefined inactive payload, never a valid active control/data value.","sampling":"Inputs change while clock low. Pre sampled at +4ns, rising edge at +5ns, post at +9ns, falling edge at +10ns. One row is one physical cycle. Initial trace has two reset cycles followed by quiescence. No extra hidden clock edges.","boundary":"Checker consumes actual public-pin observations, not model offers or driver queue ledgers. Drivers may queue offers and hold pins through acceptance. Do not trust driver internal accounting as functional truth.","environment_contract":"External masters obey pipelined acceptance, holding pending request payload through backpressure except explicit cycle abort/reset. External slaves respond in acceptance order, may wait, and do not generate unsolicited responses. Slave work is canceled on cycle withdrawal/reset. Record violations separately from DUT faults. Exact future JSON offer fields are irrelevant to the pin-based checker."}},"driver_contract":"One action is one physical clock cycle. Fields are unsigned packed integers; master 0 uses the low slice. rst is synchronous active-high reset. offer bit m queues one request for master m; addr has two 8-bit addresses, data two 32-bit payloads, we two direction bits, sel two 4-bit byte masks. The driver holds queued STB/payload until pre-edge CYC & STB & !STALL acceptance and keeps CYC high while queued or accepted work remains. A sampled ERR discards remaining accepted work for that master, preserves unaccepted offers and inserts one counted low-CYC cycle. Reset discards both kinds of work; offer must be zero when rst=1. The slave environment never stalls; it returns ACK or ERR one cycle after acceptance, gated by downstream CYC. slave_error bit s selects ERR for slave s at that response cycle. Read data is 0xa55a0000 OR the accepted address. Idle actions are real counted cycles. Pending work survives across appended rounds through cumulative replay. No automatic tail drain is added.","field_widths":{"rst":1,"offer":2,"addr":16,"data":64,"we":2,"sel":8,"slave_error":2},"rtl":{"third_party/wb2axip_candidate/rtl/skidbuffer.v":"////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
//
// Filename:	rtl/skidbuffer.v
// {{{
// Project:	WB2AXIPSP: bus bridges and other odds and ends
//
// Purpose:	A basic SKID buffer.
// {{{
//	Skid buffers are required for high throughput AXI code, since the AXI
//	specification requires that all outputs be registered.  This means
//	that, if there are any stall conditions calculated, it will take a clock
//	cycle before the stall can be propagated up stream.  This means that
//	the data will need to be buffered for a cycle until the stall signal
//	can make it to the output.
//
//	Handling that buffer is the purpose of this core.
//
//	On one end of this core, you have the i_valid and i_data inputs to
//	connect to your bus interface.  There's also a registered o_ready
//	signal to signal stalls for the bus interface.
//
//	The other end of the core has the same basic interface, but it isn't
//	registered.  This allows you to interact with the bus interfaces
//	as though they were combinatorial logic, by interacting with this half
//	of the core.
//
//	If at any time the incoming !stall signal, i_ready, signals a stall,
//	the incoming data is placed into a buffer.  Internally, that buffer
//	is held in r_data with the r_valid flag used to indicate that valid
//	data is within it.
// }}}
// Parameters:
// {{{
//	DW or data width
//		In order to make this core generic, the width of the data in the
//		skid buffer is parameterized
//
//	OPT_LOWPOWER
//		Forces both o_data and r_data to zero if the respective *VALID
//		signal is also low.  While this costs extra logic, it can also
//		be used to guarantee that any unused values aren't toggling and
//		therefore unnecessarily using power.
//
//		This excess toggling can be particularly problematic if the
//		bus signals have a high fanout rate, or a long signal path
//		across an FPGA.
//
//	OPT_OUTREG
//		Causes the outputs to be registered
//
//	OPT_PASSTHROUGH
//		Turns the skid buffer into a passthrough.  Used for formal
//		verification only.
// }}}
// Creator:	Dan Gisselquist, Ph.D.
//		Gisselquist Technology, LLC
//
////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// }}}
// Copyright (C) 2019-2025, Gisselquist Technology, LLC
// {{{
// This file is part of the WB2AXIP project.
//
// The WB2AXIP project contains free software and gateware, licensed under the
// Apache License, Version 2.0 (the "License").  You may not use this project,
// or this file, except in compliance with the License.  You may obtain a copy
// of the License at
// }}}
//	http://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0
// {{{
// Unless required by applicable law or agreed to in writing, software
// distributed under the License is distributed on an "AS IS" BASIS, WITHOUT
// WARRANTIES OR CONDITIONS OF ANY KIND, either express or implied.  See the
// License for the specific language governing permissions and limitations
// under the License.
//
////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
//
`default_nettype none
// }}}
module skidbuffer #(
		// {{{
		parameter	[0:0]	OPT_LOWPOWER = 0,
		parameter	[0:0]	OPT_OUTREG = 1,
		//
		parameter	[0:0]	OPT_PASSTHROUGH = 0,
		parameter		DW = 8,
		parameter	[0:0]	OPT_INITIAL = 1'b1
		// }}}
	) (
		// {{{
		input	wire			i_clk, i_reset,
		input	wire			i_valid,
		output	wire			o_ready,
		input	wire	[DW-1:0]	i_data,
		output	wire			o_valid,
		input	wire			i_ready,
		output	reg	[DW-1:0]	o_data
		// }}}
	);

	wire	[DW-1:0]	w_data;

	generate if (OPT_PASSTHROUGH)
	begin : PASSTHROUGH
		// {{{
		assign	{ o_valid, o_ready } = { i_valid, i_ready };

		always @(*)
		if (!i_valid && OPT_LOWPOWER)
			o_data = 0;
		else
			o_data = i_data;

		assign	w_data = 0;

		// Keep Verilator happy
		// Verilator lint_off UNUSED
		// {{{
		wire	unused_passthrough;
		assign	unused_passthrough = &{ 1'b0, i_clk, i_reset };
		// }}}
		// Verilator lint_on  UNUSED
		// }}}
	end else begin : LOGIC
		// We'll start with skid buffer itself
		// {{{
		reg			r_valid;
		reg	[DW-1:0]	r_data;

		// r_valid
		// {{{
		initial if (OPT_INITIAL) r_valid = 0;
		always @(posedge i_clk)
		if (i_reset)
			r_valid <= 0;
		else if ((i_valid && o_ready) && (o_valid && !i_ready))
			// We have incoming data, but the output is stalled
			r_valid <= 1;
		else if (i_ready)
			r_valid <= 0;
		// }}}

		// r_data
		// {{{
		initial if (OPT_INITIAL) r_data = 0;
		always @(posedge i_clk)
		if (OPT_LOWPOWER && i_reset)
			r_data <= 0;
		else if (OPT_LOWPOWER && (!o_valid || i_ready))
			r_data <= 0;
		else if ((!OPT_LOWPOWER || !OPT_OUTREG || i_valid) && o_ready)
			r_data <= i_data;

		assign	w_data = r_data;
		// }}}

		// o_ready
		// {{{
		assign o_ready = !r_valid;
		// }}}

		//
		// And then move on to the output port
		//
		if (!OPT_OUTREG)
		begin : NET_OUTPUT
			// Outputs are combinatorially determined from inputs
			// {{{
			// o_valid
			// {{{
			assign	o_valid = !i_reset && (i_valid || r_valid);
			// }}}

			// o_data
			// {{{
			always @(*)
			if (r_valid)
				o_data = r_data;
			else if (!OPT_LOWPOWER || i_valid)
				o_data = i_data;
			else
				o_data = 0;
			// }}}
			// }}}
		end else begin : REG_OUTPUT
			// Register our outputs
			// {{{
			// o_valid
			// {{{
			reg	ro_valid;

			initial if (OPT_INITIAL) ro_valid = 0;
			always @(posedge i_clk)
			if (i_reset)
				ro_valid <= 0;
			else if (!o_valid || i_ready)
				ro_valid <= (i_valid || r_valid);

			assign	o_valid = ro_valid;
			// }}}

			// o_data
			// {{{
			initial if (OPT_INITIAL) o_data = 0;
			always @(posedge i_clk)
			if (OPT_LOWPOWER && i_reset)
				o_data <= 0;
			else if (!o_valid || i_ready)
			begin

				if (r_valid)
					o_data <= r_data;
				else if (!OPT_LOWPOWER || i_valid)
					o_data <= i_data;
				else
					o_data <= 0;
			end
			// }}}

			// }}}
		end
		// }}}
	end endgenerate

	// Keep Verilator happy
	// {{{
	// verilator coverage_off
	// Verilator lint_off UNUSED
	wire	unused;
	assign	unused = &{ 1'b0, w_data };
	// Verilator lint_on  UNUSED
	// verilator coverage_on
	// }}}
////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
//
// Formal properties
// {{{
////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
`ifdef	FORMAL
`ifdef	SKIDBUFFER
`define	ASSUME	assume
`else
`define	ASSUME	assert
`endif

	reg	f_past_valid;

	initial	f_past_valid = 0;
	always @(posedge i_clk)
		f_past_valid <= 1;

	always @(*)
	if (!f_past_valid)
		assume(i_reset);

	////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
	//
	// Incoming stream properties / assumptions
	// {{{
	////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
	//
	always @(posedge i_clk)
	if (!f_past_valid)
	begin
		`ASSUME(!i_valid || !OPT_INITIAL);
	end else if ($past(i_valid && !o_ready && !i_reset) && !i_reset)
		`ASSUME(i_valid && $stable(i_data));

`ifdef	VERIFIC
`define	FORMAL_VERIFIC
	// Reset properties
	property RESET_CLEARS_IVALID;
		@(posedge i_clk) i_reset |=> !i_valid;
	endproperty

	property IDATA_HELD_WHEN_NOT_READY;
		@(posedge i_clk) disable iff (i_reset)
		i_valid && !o_ready |=> i_valid && $stable(i_data);
	endproperty

`ifdef	SKIDBUFFER
	assume	property (IDATA_HELD_WHEN_NOT_READY);
`else
	assert	property (IDATA_HELD_WHEN_NOT_READY);
`endif
`endif
	// }}}
	////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
	//
	// Outgoing stream properties / assumptions
	// {{{
	////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
	//

	generate if (!OPT_PASSTHROUGH)
	begin

		always @(posedge i_clk)
		if (!f_past_valid) // || $past(i_reset))
		begin
			// Following any reset, valid must be deasserted
			assert(!o_valid || !OPT_INITIAL);
		end else if ($past(o_valid && !i_ready && !i_reset) && !i_reset)
			// Following any stall, valid must remain high and
			// data must be preserved
			assert(o_valid && $stable(o_data));

	end endgenerate
	// }}}
	////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
	//
	// Other properties
	// {{{
	////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
	//
	//
	generate if (!OPT_PASSTHROUGH)
	begin
		// Rule #1:
		//	If registered, then following any reset we should be
		//	ready for a new request
		// {{{
		always @(posedge i_clk)
		if (f_past_valid && $past(OPT_OUTREG && i_reset))
			assert(o_ready);
		// }}}

		// Rule #2:
		//	All incoming data must either go directly to the
		//	output port, or into the skid buffer
		// {{{
`ifndef	VERIFIC
		always @(posedge i_clk)
		if (f_past_valid && !$past(i_reset) && $past(i_valid && o_ready
			&& (!OPT_OUTREG || o_valid) && !i_ready))
			assert(!o_ready && w_data == $past(i_data));
`else
		assert property (@(posedge i_clk)
			disable iff (i_reset)
			(i_valid && o_ready
				&& (!OPT_OUTREG || o_valid) && !i_ready)
				|=> (!o_ready && w_data == $past(i_data)));
`endif
		// }}}

		// Rule #3:
		//	After the last transaction, o_valid should become idle
		// {{{
		if (!OPT_OUTREG)
		begin
			// {{{
			always @(posedge i_clk)
			if (f_past_valid && !$past(i_reset) && !i_reset
					&& $past(i_ready))
			begin
				assert(o_valid == i_valid);
				assert(!i_valid || (o_data == i_data));
			end
			// }}}
		end else begin
			// {{{
			always @(posedge i_clk)
			if (f_past_valid && !$past(i_reset))
			begin
				if ($past(i_valid && o_ready))
					assert(o_valid);

				if ($past(!i_valid && o_ready && i_ready))
					assert(!o_valid);
			end
			// }}}
		end
		// }}}

		// Rule #4
		//	Same thing, but this time for o_ready
		// {{{
		always @(posedge i_clk)
		if (f_past_valid && $past(!o_ready && i_ready))
			assert(o_ready);
		// }}}

		// If OPT_LOWPOWER is set, o_data and w_data both need to be
		// zero any time !o_valid or !r_valid respectively
		// {{{
		if (OPT_LOWPOWER)
		begin
			always @(*)
			if ((OPT_OUTREG || !i_reset) && !o_valid)
				assert(o_data == 0);

			always @(*)
			if (o_ready)
				assert(w_data == 0);

		end
		// }}}
	end endgenerate
	// }}}

	always @(posedge i_clk)
	if (!OPT_PASSTHROUGH && !i_reset && !o_ready)
		assert(o_valid);

	////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
	//
	// Cover checks
	// {{{
	////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
	//
	//
`ifdef	SKIDBUFFER
	generate if (!OPT_PASSTHROUGH)
	begin
		reg	f_changed_data;

		initial	f_changed_data = 0;
		always @(posedge i_clk)
		if (i_reset)
			f_changed_data <= 1;
		else if (i_valid && $past(!i_valid || o_ready))
		begin
			if (i_data != $past(i_data + 1))
				f_changed_data <= 0;
		end else if (!i_valid && i_data != 0)
			f_changed_data <= 0;


`ifndef	VERIFIC
		reg	[3:0]	cvr_steps, cvr_hold;

		always @(posedge i_clk)
		if (i_reset)
		begin
			cvr_steps <= 0;
			cvr_hold  <= 0;
		end else begin
			cvr_steps <= cvr_steps + 1;
			cvr_hold  <= cvr_hold  + 1;
			case(cvr_steps)
			 0: if (o_valid || i_valid)
				cvr_steps <= 0;
			 1: if (!i_valid || !i_ready)
				cvr_steps <= 0;
			 2: if (!i_valid || !i_ready)
				cvr_steps <= 0;
			 3: if (!i_valid || !i_ready)
				cvr_steps <= 0;
			 4: if (!i_valid ||  i_ready)
				cvr_steps <= 0;
			 5: if (!i_valid || !i_ready)
				cvr_steps <= 0;
			 6: if (!i_valid || !i_ready)
				cvr_steps <= 0;
			 7: if (!i_valid ||  i_ready)
				cvr_steps <= 0;
			 8: if (!i_valid ||  i_ready)
				cvr_steps <= 0;
			 9: if (!i_valid || !i_ready)
				cvr_steps <= 0;
			10: if (!i_valid || !i_ready)
				cvr_steps <= 0;
			11: if (!i_valid || !i_ready)
				cvr_steps <= 0;
			12: begin
				cvr_steps <= cvr_steps;
				cover(!o_valid && !i_valid && f_changed_data);
				if (!o_valid || !i_ready)
					cvr_steps <= 0;
				else
					cvr_hold <= cvr_hold + 1;
				end
			default: assert(0);
			endcase
		end

`else
		// Cover test
		cover property (@(posedge i_clk)
			disable iff (i_reset)
			(!o_valid && !i_valid)
			##1 i_valid &&  i_ready [*3]
			##1 i_valid && !i_ready
			##1 i_valid &&  i_ready [*2]
			##1 i_valid && !i_ready [*2]
			##1 i_valid &&  i_ready [*3]
			// Wait for the design to clear
			##1 o_valid && i_ready [*0:5]
			##1 (!o_valid && !i_valid && f_changed_data));
`endif
	end endgenerate
`endif	// SKIDBUFFER
	// }}}
`endif
// }}}
endmodule
","third_party/wb2axip_candidate/rtl/addrdecode.v":"////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
//
// Filename:	rtl/addrdecode.v
// {{{
// Project:	WB2AXIPSP: bus bridges and other odds and ends
//
// Purpose:	Supports bus crossbars by answering the question, which slave
//		does the current address need to be routed to?  Requests are
//	pipelined using valid/!stall handshaking.  For those familiar with
//	AXI, READY=!STALL.  The outgoing stream is identical to the incoming
//	one, save for the (new) o_decode field.  This is a bitmask containing
//	one bit for each slave that the request might be routed to, and one
//	extra bit to indicate no slaves matched.
//
//	The keys to the operation of this module are found in the two
//	parameters, SLAVE_ADDR and SLAVE_MASK.
//
//	SLAVE_ADDR specifies the address of the slave in question.  It's a large
//		array, with one address (i.e. one set of AW bits) for each
//		potential slave address region.
//
//	SLAVE_MASK specifies which of the bits in SLAVE_ADDR need to match in
//		order to route a request to a that slave.
//
//	It's important to guarantee that no two slaves will ever map to the
//	same address, and likewise any given slave may only map to a single
//	address region.
//
//	Incidentally, the algorithm forces all slaves to have an address
//	aligned with their memory size.  Hence a 2GB memory must have an
//	address (range) of either 0-2GB, 2GB-4GB, 4GB-6GB, etc.  However, a
//	second slave having only 8kB of memory may be placed at 0-8kB,
//	8-16kB, 16-24kB, etc.  For logic minimization purposes, it is often to
//	the advantage of the bus compositor to minimize the number of mask
//	bits, and hence 8kB slaves may be aliased to many places in memory.
//	Bus composition and address assignment, however, are both outside of
//	the scope of the operation of this module.
//	
//
// Creator:	Dan Gisselquist, Ph.D.
//		Gisselquist Technology, LLC
//
////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// }}}
// Copyright (C) 2019-2025, Gisselquist Technology, LLC
